.end

.h2 How this manual is written [view-conventions]
.ix Keycap, notation of keys
.ix Typographical conventions
.ix Terminal and X11, differences
.ix Behavior of Strata
.ix Note and Caution paragraphs
.ix Menu path, notation
.ix Program font, notation
.ix Messages, notation of
.ix Section sign, cross reference
.ix Appendix, cross reference
Keys are shown as keycaps: {k:Ctrl-S} means that you hold the {k:Ctrl} key
and press {k:S}; {k:Alt-F12} means {k:Alt} and the function key {k:F12};
{k:Shift-Up} means {k:Shift} and the up arrow.  The keys of the numeric
keypad are written {k:Keypad +}, {k:Keypad -} and {k:Keypad *}.  The key
that ends a line is {k:Enter}; the key that accepts a panel is {k:Ins}
(also called {k:Insert}).  A key that is written with a capital letter
after {k:Ctrl-} or {k:Alt-} is the same key whether or not the Shift key
is down; the Editor treats {k:Ctrl-s} and {k:Ctrl-S} as the same key.

A command that is on the {k:F12} menu is written as a path: File, Save means
the command Save of the menu File.  In the menus and the panels the words
that are on the screen are written exactly as they are on the screen,
with their capital letters.  The name of a field or of a panel is in bold
where its description begins.

File names, directories, commands of the system, settings and the values
that a setting takes are in the program font, as in {c:config} and
{c:print_to}.  A message of a program is in double quotes, exactly as it is
shown; an italic word inside it stands for text that varies, as in "Delete
{i:name}?".  A measure is a number, a space and the unit (7 mm, 14 pt), and
a number has no thousands separator (65000).

The names LaTeX and TeX are printed with their logos, as the name Strata is,
in the text, the headlines, the contents, the tables and the captions.  The
name in a message in double quotes, in the program font and in an entry of
the index is plain text, because it is the text of a program or of a list.

A reference to a chapter or a section is the section sign and the number,
with the title in parentheses, as in {x:file}.  A reference to a number
alone, in a place where a title would nest inside parentheses, has the sign
and the number only, as in {n:file}.  A reference to an appendix or to a
section of one has the word Appendix in place of the sign: {x:appcfg}.
Two or more appendices in a row have the plural word once, as in {n:appcmd}
and {n:appx11}, and the sections of one appendix have the singular word once,
as in {n:appcfg-format} and {n:appcfg-keys}.

The description of a marker has the same parts every time: a table of the
panel, the sections On the screen and When printed, and the section Edges for
what happens at a limit or in an odd place.  The description of a command of
the File menu has the section for its panel, the section for its edges and, as
the last section, the section See, which lists the other places that bear on
the command.  The index lists a second name of a thing with the words See and
the name that the manual uses.

Strata comes in two versions of the Editor: a terminal version that
runs in a text terminal, and an X11 version that opens a window.  Where
the two differ, the text says so.  {n:appterm} and {n:appx11} describe the
terminal version and the X11 version in that order; a short table of the
differences is in {x:view-screen-kinds}.

Three kinds of paragraph are set apart.  A {b:Note} adds a fact to the text
above it.  A {b:Caution} warns of a loss of work or of a surprise.  A
paragraph that begins {b:Behavior of Strata} describes a detail of the
program that the rest of the text does not make obvious.

.note Strata is one program for two displays.  A screen in this manual is
  drawn from the X11 version unless the text says that it is the terminal.
  The terminal shows the same screen with the characters of your terminal;
  the colors are your terminal's own.

